course: dictionary
lesson: 14675
title: A special right
level: primary-6
stars: 3
explain: A prerogative is a special right. It belongs to a person or group because of who they are or the job they do. «It is the judge's prerogative to decide.» Only the judge has this right, because of the job the judge does.
explain: Prerogative is a noun, and its plural is prerogatives. In history and citizenship lessons, royal prerogative means the powers that belong to the king or queen, and to the government acting for them.
explain: In everyday talk, people use the word in a slightly formal way to mean a choice that nobody else can make for you. «It's your prerogative to change your mind.» Nobody can make that decision for you.
explain: To find the meaning, look at who has the right and why. The clues are often the person's job, title or role. Then try your meaning in the sentence: if «special right» fits, you have it.
fact: Prerogative means a special right that belongs to someone because of who they are or their job.
fact: «Royal prerogative» means powers that belong to the king or queen and the government acting for them.
fact: In everyday talk, «It's your prerogative» means it is your own choice, and nobody else can make it for you.
fact: Prerogative is a noun. Its plural is prerogatives.
exercise: «The judge decided the case. Deciding it was the judge's prerogative.» What does prerogative mean here? | A loud noise made in a courtroom | *A special right that belongs to the judge because of the job | A kind of old hat | Something you pay for at the shops
exercise: «It's your prerogative to change your mind.» What does this mean? | You must change your mind when someone tells you to | *It is your own choice, and nobody else can make it for you | Your mind is very small | You have lost your mind
exercise: What does «royal prerogative» mean? | A crown made of gold | A holiday for the royal family | A sad feeling about the king | *Powers that belong to the king or queen and the government acting for them
test: «Only the head teacher has the prerogative to close the school early.» What does prerogative mean in this sentence? | A school bell | *A special right that belongs to the head teacher because of the job | A cup of tea
test: Which sentence uses prerogative to mean a choice nobody else can make for you? | *«Whether you wear a hat is your prerogative.» | «She had a prerogative cake for lunch.» | «The prerogative bus was late.» | «The prerogative is in the cupboard.»
exam: Which word is the best meaning of prerogative? | A small animal | Something you borrow | A loud shout | *A special right that belongs to someone
